"""Render the Week 2 method-and-baseline report from computed evidence."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,json
from xml.sax.saxutils import escape
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate,Paragraph,Table,TableStyle,Spacer,PageBreak,Image
from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];O=R/'docs/step_2/week_2';O.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
S=json.loads((R/'results/week_2/execution_summary.json').read_text())
def rows(name):return list(csv.DictReader((R/'results/week_2'/name).open()))
styles=getSampleStyleSheet();styles['Normal'].fontSize=10;styles['Normal'].leading=13;styles['Normal'].spaceAfter=8
for name in ['Heading1','Heading2']:styles[name].textColor=colors.HexColor('#123F46')
story=[];markdown=['# Method and Baseline\n\nSowmya | Remote Patient Health Monitoring Using Machine Learning\n']
def h(t):story.append(Paragraph(escape(t),styles['Heading2']));markdown.append('## '+t+'\n')
def p(t):story.append(Paragraph(escape(t),styles['Normal']));markdown.append(t+'\n')
def table(headers,data,widths):
 t=Table([headers]+data,colWidths=widths,repeatRows=1)
 t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#123F46')),('TEXTCOLOR',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.white),('FONTNAME',(0,0),(-1,0),'Helvetica-Bold'),('FONTSIZE',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7),('ROWBACKGROUNDS',(0,1),(-1,-1),[colors.HexColor('#EDF5F4'),colors.white])]))
 story.extend([t,Spacer(1,10)]);markdown.append('| '+' | '.join(headers)+' |\n| '+' | '.join(['---']*len(headers))+' |\n'+'\n'.join('| '+' | '.join(map(str,row))+' |' for row in data)+'\n')
def page():story.append(PageBreak())
story.append(Paragraph('Method and Baseline',styles['Title']))
p('Sowmya | Step 2, Week 2 | November 3, 2026')
p('Remote Patient Health Monitoring Using Machine Learning: an offline ECG classification study. This milestone demonstrates an evidence-producing computational process, not a completed research investigation.')
h('1. Computational method implemented')
p('My research question is: How do Gaussian Naive Bayes and Random Forest compare in classifying selected MIT-BIH ECG heartbeat classes under a subject-disjoint evaluation design? My working hypothesis remains nondirectional: the classifiers may differ in macro-F1 and class-specific precision and recall.')
p('I implemented an initial binary comparison using original annotation symbols N (normal beat) and V (premature ventricular contraction). These are not full AAMI superclasses. All other symbols are excluded as prediction targets. Expert annotations provide beat locations and the previous reference-beat interval; automatic beat detection is outside this experiment.')
p('I select MLII by name and require 360 Hz. A beat window contains 90 samples before the annotation and 126 samples from the annotation onward, with the upper endpoint excluded: 216 samples or 0.6 seconds. I exclude absent preceding beats, incomplete windows and nonfinite or constant windows. No filtering, imputation or oversampling is applied.')
p('My 27 features contain the previous reference-beat interval in seconds, raw window standard deviation and peak-to-peak amplitude, and 24 linearly interpolated values from a per-window z-normalized waveform. Previous timing uses all recognized reference beat types, not rhythm/noise annotations; excluded target types can still delimit an interval. Feature extraction does not receive the target label.')
h('2. Source code responsible for the method')
p('src/week1_ecg_research_foundation.ipynb preserves my original Week 1 cells and appends Week 2 setup, acquisition, segmentation/features, validation, model comparison, evidence/repeatability, and observations. The complete Week 2 code is also in src/run_week2_ecg.py. src/week2_protocol.json fixes the partitions, targets, features, settings and metrics before scoring. src/requirements_week2.txt records the tested packages. src/build_week2_report.py renders this report from generated results.')
h('3. Baseline and reference conditions')
p('A DummyClassifier using the most frequent training class is the simple reference. Gaussian Naive Bayes and Random Forest use the same training and evaluation rows and features. Each learned-model pipeline fits StandardScaler on training data only. This avoids fitting a population transform to evaluation features; per-window z-normalization is a fixed operation within each individual waveform.')
page();h('4. Experimental procedure and fixed parameters')
p('I selected a small convenience subset expanding the Week 1 foundation. Training records are 106, 108 and 119; evaluation records are 100, 105 and 200. These six records are from different subjects. Records 201 and 202 share a subject and are excluded; my grouping helper also assigns them a common group defensively. I do not randomly split beats or claim to implement the full standard inter-patient benchmark.')
p('The versioned PhysioNet source is MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database 1.0.0. For each record I acquire .hea, .dat and .atr files, record local SHA-256 hashes, and verify signal/annotation structure. The hashes record the acquired bytes; they have not been independently verified against provider checksums. Raw files are excluded from Git and can be downloaded by the code.')
table(['Partition','Record','N beats','V beats'],[['Train','106','1506','520'],['Train','108','1738','17'],['Train','119','1542','444'],['Evaluation','100','2237','1'],['Evaluation','105','2525','41'],['Evaluation','200','1742','825']],[105,100,120,120])
p('The resulting feature table contains 5,767 training beats and 7,371 evaluation beats. Eight N/V annotations were excluded: six lacking a preceding reference beat and two with incomplete windows. All other annotation types are counted separately and excluded as targets rather than relabeled N.')
p('GaussianNB uses var_smoothing=1e-9. RandomForestClassifier uses 100 trees, max_depth=12, min_samples_leaf=2, class_weight=balanced, random_state=42 and n_jobs=1. These settings and the partition were fixed before scoring. There is no hyperparameter search, cross-validation or resampling. Record 100 was inspected descriptively in Week 1, so I do not treat this development evaluation as an untouched final holdout.')
p('Primary evidence is pooled macro-F1 over fixed labels [N,V], plus each class precision, recall, F1 and support. I also record accuracy, balanced accuracy, confusion matrices and per-record macro-F1. Undefined precision/recall is set to zero. Pooled metrics weight beats equally; they do not give each subject equal weight. Per-record results retain both labels in macro-F1 regardless of support.')
h('5. Execute and reproduce the experiment')
p('Use a dedicated Python environment. From the repository root run: python -m pip install -r src/requirements_week2.txt ; then python src/run_week2_ecg.py . The commands are separate steps. Alternatively open the notebook from the repository root or src folder and run its Week 2 section in order. Initial downloads require internet and about 12 MB. Cached nonempty raw files are reused.')
p('The standalone script runs Week 2 without overwriting Week 1 artifacts. A full notebook rerun includes the original Week 1 cells, which recreate their outputs and environment files as documented there; skip the optional Week 1 installation cell when dependencies are configured. Run python src/build_week2_report.py after results exist to regenerate this report.')
page();h('6. Initial experimental results')
table(['Method','Accuracy','Balanced acc.','Macro-F1'],[[r['model'].replace('_',' '),f"{float(r['accuracy']):.6f}",f"{float(r['balanced_accuracy']):.6f}",f"{float(r['macro_f1']):.6f}"] for r in rows('metrics_summary.csv')],[130,100,115,100])
p('On this fixed partition, Random Forest macro-F1 exceeded Gaussian Naive Bayes by 0.038002. Gaussian Naive Bayes had higher accuracy. The majority reference achieved 88.24% accuracy while detecting no V beats, demonstrating the limitation of accuracy alone for this imbalanced evaluation.')
table(['Method','V precision','V recall','V F1'],[[r['model'].replace('_',' '),f"{float(r['precision']):.6f}",f"{float(r['recall']):.6f}",f"{float(r['f1']):.6f}"] for r in rows('metrics_by_class.csv') if r['class']=='V'],[130,105,105,105])
p('Gaussian Naive Bayes correctly identified 402 of 867 V beats, missed 465, and incorrectly labeled 116 N beats as V. Random Forest identified 855 V beats, missed 12, and incorrectly labeled 796 N beats as V. Random Forest therefore improved V recall at a substantial false-positive cost. Its balanced class weights are part of the configuration, so this does not isolate architecture alone.')
story.append(Image(str(R/'results/week_2/confusion_matrices.png'),width=460,height=152));markdown.append('![Confusion matrices](../../../results/week_2/confusion_matrices.png)\n')
p('The result was not consistent across all subjects. GaussianNB / Random Forest macro-F1 was 1.000000 / 0.833222 on record 100, 0.601660 / 0.537161 on record 105, and 0.753942 / 0.840734 on record 200. Record 100 has only one V beat. Record 200 contributes 825 of the 867 evaluation V beats, so pooled minority-class results depend heavily on that subject.')
p('metrics_summary.csv, metrics_by_class.csv, metrics_by_record.csv and confusion_matrices.csv hold the computed values. predictions.npz stores every evaluation record ID, annotation sample, true label and model prediction. Load it with numpy.load(..., allow_pickle=False); all arrays have the same row order. No synthetic examples or invented classifier scores were used.')
page();h('7. Validation checks performed')
p('All 30 automated checks passed. Pre-fit checks cover known feature length, previous interval, analytic standard deviation, peak-to-peak amplitude and morphology endpoints; invalid/constant/nonfinite window and interval rejection; same-subject 201/202 grouping; record/subject separation; unique record/sample keys; finite features; both classes in each partition; exclusion accounting; and known macro-F1/confusion-matrix examples.')
p('Post-fit checks verify valid predictions and confusion totals for all three methods, training-only scaler statistics and fit counts for both learned models, unchanged protocol bytes, and identical Random Forest predictions after a same-seed refit. The refit checks repeatability, not statistical uncertainty. Validation results are recorded in validation_checks.csv. Checks do not independently establish annotation accuracy or physiological quality.')
h('8. Methodological problems and unresolved questions')
p('Only three training and three evaluation subjects are included. Within-subject beats are correlated, and the V class is concentrated in one evaluation subject. Neither the large beat count nor a same-seed refit establishes population-level generalization or statistical superiority. Full-database, independent and external validation remain unperformed.')
p('Only original N and V symbols are modeled; other beat categories, paced beats and artifacts are not classified. Reference annotations provide both center locations and timing. The post-beat window and oracle timing prevent treating this as a real-time detector or monitoring system. No clinical diagnosis, alarm behavior or heart-attack prediction is evaluated.')
p('My next priorities are to add subjects with meaningful V support, examine record-level false positives and misses, define a validation partition before changing parameters or features, and assess subject-level variability when the number of subjects permits. These are planned Week 3 tasks. The present evidence demonstrates a functioning method with preliminary differences and trade-offs rather than a final winning classifier.')
h('Execution provenance and references')
p('Executed Python '+S['python']+'; '+', '.join(k+' '+v for k,v in S['packages'].items())+'. execution_summary.json records the protocol, hashes, feature names, timing and environment. Timing is descriptive and environment-dependent. The saved Week 2 execution is from the recorded preparation environment; exact bitwise reproduction across systems is not guaranteed.')
p('Moody, G., & Mark, R. (2005). MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database (v1.0.0). PhysioNet. https://doi.org/10.13026/C2F305 . Database description: https://physionet.org/content/mitdb/1.0.0/ . Annotation definitions: https://physionet.org/physiobank/database/html/mitdbdir/intro.htm . WFDB reading API: https://wfdb.readthedocs.io/en/latest/io.html . scikit-learn model documentation: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/naive_bayes.html and https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/ensemble.html#forest .')
def footer(c,d):
 c.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor('#C8D8D8'));c.line(48,44,564,44);c.setFillColor(colors.HexColor('#426366'));c.setFont('Helvetica',8);c.drawString(48,30,'Sowmya | COMP 699 A | Week 2 preliminary computational evidence');c.drawRightString(564,30,str(d.page))
SimpleDocTemplate(str(O/'method_and_baseline.pdf'),pagesize=(612,792),leftMargin=48,rightMargin=48,topMargin=40,bottomMargin=58).build(story,onFirstPage=footer,onLaterPages=footer)
(O/'method_and_baseline.md').write_text('\n'.join(markdown))
print(O/'method_and_baseline.pdf')
